</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Information Theory Analysis of the Different Algorithms</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A common point of confusion in deep learning at an early age is how neural networks with so many parameters still have generalization ability<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. The normal statistical model uses an extremely limited number of parameters to ensure that it can concisely describe the phenomenon and capture the essence of the observed data. For this problem, the study theory<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> claims that limiting the number of functions that a model can express helps ensure that the model approaches the best functions it can have, and that it also bounds the distance between the final function a neural network study about and the average of functions. Generally, a possible function expressed by a model is a hypothesis <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, and if the number of input data points is <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of possible hypotheses is less than <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. According to the information theory, for a dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with data <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, the typical set that a model can gain access to is <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{H(D)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{H(D)}</annotation></semantics></math><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. Considering the amount of data in the typical set, and the mutual information between the output and the middle feature of the neural network <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(y,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(y,z)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can further bound the models’ capacity for potential hypotheses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Explicitly, we can consider the probability of middle feature <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> given output <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z|y)</annotation></semantics></math> provides a match between <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>. The information bottleneck theory<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> shows that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{H(Y|Z)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{H(Y|Z)}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the volume of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> project to the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{I(Y,Z)}=\frac{2^{H(Y)}}{2^{H(Y|Z)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{I(Y,Z)}=\frac{2^{H(Y)}}{2^{H(Y|Z)}}</annotation></semantics></math> further constraints the possible hypotheses. As the mutual information bounds the distance between the study hypothesis and the average hypothesis, generative adversarial networks (GANs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, which face mode collapse<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> that only generate a subset of the dataset, can benefit from it by adding a mutual information constraint <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. One study<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> shows that this phenomenon also appears in the diffusion distillation, which consistently changes the score function and triggers an increase in mutual information. The basic idea of the proof in that study is related to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-variation auto-encoder (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-VAE)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, in which, when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-VAE decreases <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> to let VAE generate high-quality images, the upper bounce of the mutual information constraint will be abated, and there will be an increase in the peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) of the encoding feature <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. From this perspective, the diffusion model can be deemed as a hierarchy VAE<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, while progressive distillation<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> increases the mutual information by letting the model directly use <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\theta}({t+2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\theta}({t+2})</annotation></semantics></math> to replace the original output <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\theta}(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\theta}(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is a noisy feature output by solving ODE. Hence, the PSNR also increases. A recent example of diffusion distillation is SDXL-lightning<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, which adds GAN loss to the progressive distillation training, and its FID remains similar to normal progressive distillation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, it is intuitive that changing the direction of the score function to allow it to point to the final result, rather than the centroid of the possible image, will also affect the mutual information, even if the output does not have an increased PSNR. We provide further analysis regarding this.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The diffusion ODE can be regarded as a Markov process <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\theta}(T)-X_{\theta}(T-1)-...-X_{\theta}(t)-...X_{\theta}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\theta}(T)-X_{\theta}(T-1)-...-X_{\theta}(t)-...X_{\theta}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, in which <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\theta}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\theta}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> follows the input of the diffusion model predicted by the U-Net, and we have the following <a href="#S3.E23" title="In 3.2 Information Theory Analysis of the Different Algorithms ‣ 3 Approach ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(X_{\theta}(0),X_{\theta}(t))=H(X_{\theta}(0))-H(X_{\theta}(0)|X_{\theta}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(X_{\theta}(0),X_{\theta}(t))=H(X_{\theta}(0))-H(X_{\theta}(0)|X_{\theta}(t))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq H(X(0))-E_{X_{\theta}(t)}[p(X_{\theta}(0)|X_{\theta}(t)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq H(X(0))-E_{X_{\theta}(t)}[p(X_{\theta}(0)|X_{\theta}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle*\log p_{\theta}(X_{\theta}(0)|X_{\theta}(t)))]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle*\log p_{\theta}(X_{\theta}(0)|X_{\theta}(t)))],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">in which <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X(0))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X(0))</annotation></semantics></math> is the real <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(0)</annotation></semantics></math> sample from the dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-VAE, when given the random variable <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, the output <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed to be sampled from a Gaussian distribution using <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> as its mean, and abating the <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> makes the middle feature <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> more similar to <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> while enhancing the log likelihood of the final synthesis. Moreover, <a href="#S3.E23" title="In 3.2 Information Theory Analysis of the Different Algorithms ‣ 3 Approach ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> shows that if we modify the <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\theta}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\theta}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> to make it similar to the final output to enhance the log-likelihood by rectifying the trajectory, then the upper bound of the mutual information will also increase. Most distillation methods that do not increase the PSNR of the output will still face this problem. We evaluate the Precision and Recall of Distribution (PRD)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> of flash diffusion and our sampler to further validate this claim.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Performance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we employ <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=7</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}=1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}=1.2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="stop=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">stop=3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=8</annotation></semantics></math> as the hyperparameters of the discrete setting in the eight-step inference when generating 1024 x 1024 and 512 x 512 images. For the eight-step inference, as the precise discretization method, we can completely avoid using free-U, which can enhance the aesthetic of images while reducing its FID in the few-step inference. We report the results of the eight-step inference in the COCO 2014, COCO 2017, and LAION dataset in <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#S4.T2" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and <a href="#S4.T3" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Moreover, we use <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}=1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}=1.2</annotation></semantics></math> as the setting in the five- and six-step inference of generating 512 x 512 images and the six-step inference of generating 1024 x 1024 images. The result of the five- and six-step inference that synthesizes 512 x 512 images is also given in <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#S4.T2" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and <a href="#S4.T3" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Moreover, the result of the 1024 x 1024 images is given in <a href="#S4.T4" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <a href="#S4.T5" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, and <a href="#S4.T6" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. The free-U is used throughout the inference when we sample a 512 x 512 image, and it is used in the last three steps when we synthesize a 1024 x 1024 image. Moreover, the ODE solver, based on the analysis in Section 3, is DPM++2m for eight-step inference and DPM++1s for five-step inference. The inference model that uses our algorithm for synthesizing 512 x 512 images and 1024 x 1024 images is Stable Diffusion v1.5 and Dreamshaper XL, respectively. In the generation of 1024 x 1024 images in 6 steps, we use the SDE-Editing method mentioned in the paper<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, all the synthesized images in <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T4" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> employs the random 10k COCO caption as a guidance prompt. In <a href="#S4.T2" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T5" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, for each image in the COCO 2017 dataset, we fetch one particular caption of it as guidance. Furthermore, we use 10k captions in LAION for <a href="#S4.T3" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T6" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. We also compare the FID performance and the clip score of AMED-Pulging solver, progressive distillation, SDXL turbo, SDXL-Lightning, Flash DiffusionXL, Flash Diffusion, and DMD2<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. Our approach outperforms state-of-the-art models for most of the datasets. However, some of the models<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> are trained in LAION, which do not separate the validation and the training sets, and may affect the comparison. The <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> in the <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T4" title="In 4.1 Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Hyperparameters are all you need: Using five-step inference for an original diffusion model to generate images comparable to the latest distillation model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is the guidance scale of inference, and the number in the parentheses is the total number of function evaluations (NFEs) of the model. The <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Free-U decorator is 1.1, 1.1, 0.9, 0.2.</p>
</div>
